IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `uc-core`
IMPORT `uc2-part6-ch3-unearned-income`

-- The Universal Credit Regulations 2013 (S.I. 2013/376), Part 6, Chapter 1 (regulations 45 to 50): CAPITAL.
-- Source: ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2013-376.txt (lines 845-876), a plain-text rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text.
-- Target: the stable build 1.6.98. Money is a NUMBER of pounds; a calculated amount is rounded once, at the end, with the keel's
-- `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny` (regulation 6(1)). The module imports Chapter 3 for the yield of regulation 72,
-- which regulation 50(3)(b) uses.

§ `Part 6 -- Calculation of capital and income`

§§ `Part 6 and Chapter 1 headings; regulation 45 -- Introduction (inert)`
-- src:845 | PART 6 — CALCULATION OF CAPITAL AND INCOME
-- src:846 | CHAPTER 1 — CAPITAL
-- src:847 | 45 Introduction
-- src:848 | This Chapter provides for the calculation of a person's capital for the purpose of section 5 of the Act (financial conditions) and section 8 of the Act (calculation of awards).
-- INERT: the Part and Chapter headings and a statement of what the Chapter is for. It cites sections 5 and 8 of the Act (financial
-- conditions; calculation of awards), which are not inputs, and decides nothing a person could ask.

§ `Part 6, Chapter 1 -- Capital`

§§ `Regulation 46 -- What is included in capital?`

-- Regulation 46 sorts a sum four ways. A personal possession is not capital (2). A sum paid regularly and by reference to a period
-- is income, even if it would otherwise be capital (3), "subject to paragraph (4)"; capital payable by instalments has each
-- instalment treated as income if the amount outstanding together with the person's other capital (and the other member of a
-- couple's) exceeds 16,000 pounds, otherwise as capital (4). All other capital is taken into account unless it is disregarded
-- (1) (see regulation 48). The order below is the order the text's own cross-references force: (2), then (4) where there are
-- instalments, then (3), then (1).
-- src:850 | (1) The whole of a person's capital is to be taken into account unless—
-- src:851 | (a) it is to be treated as income (see paragraphs (3) and (4)); or
-- src:852 | (b) it is to be disregarded (see regulation 48).
-- src:853 | (2) A person's personal possessions are not to be treated as capital.
-- src:854 | (3) Subject to paragraph (4), any sums that are paid regularly and by reference to a period, for example payments under an annuity, are to be treated as income even if they would, apart from this provision, be regarded as capital or as having a capital element.
-- src:855 | (4) Where capital is payable by instalments, each payment of an instalment is to be treated as income if the amount outstanding, combined with any other capital of the person (and, if the person is a member of a couple, the other member), exceeds £16,000, but otherwise such payments are to be treated as capital.
@ref reg 46 (src:850-855)
DECLARE `Treatment of a sum under regulation 46` IS ONE OF
    `not treated as capital because it is a personal possession`
    `treated as income`
    `capital that is disregarded`
    `capital that is taken into account`

-- src:850 | (1) The whole of a person's capital is to be taken into account unless—
-- src:851 | (a) it is to be treated as income (see paragraphs (3) and (4)); or
-- src:852 | (b) it is to be disregarded (see regulation 48).
-- src:853 | (2) A person's personal possessions are not to be treated as capital.
-- src:854 | (3) Subject to paragraph (4), any sums that are paid regularly and by reference to a period, for example payments under an annuity, are to be treated as income even if they would, apart from this provision, be regarded as capital or as having a capital element.
-- src:855 | (4) Where capital is payable by instalments, each payment of an instalment is to be treated as income if the amount outstanding, combined with any other capital of the person (and, if the person is a member of a couple, the other member), exceeds £16,000, but otherwise such payments are to be treated as capital.
@ref reg 46 (src:850-855)
DECLARE `Sum considered under regulation 46` HAS
    `personal possession`                                                                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `sum paid regularly and by reference to a period`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `capital payable by instalments`                                                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `amount of the capital outstanding`                                                     IS A NUMBER
    `other capital of the person and, if the person is a member of a couple, of the other member`  IS A NUMBER
    `capital to be disregarded under regulation 48`                                         IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:855 | (4) Where capital is payable by instalments, each payment of an instalment is to be treated as income if the amount outstanding, combined with any other capital of the person (and, if the person is a member of a couple, the other member), exceeds £16,000, but otherwise such payments are to be treated as capital.
@ref reg 46(4) (src:855)
GIVEN sum IS A `Sum considered under regulation 46`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reg 46(4) — each instalment is treated as income` sum MEANS
    ((sum's `amount of the capital outstanding`) PLUS (sum's `other capital of the person and, if the person is a member of a couple, of the other member`)) GREATER THAN 16000

-- src:850 | (1) The whole of a person's capital is to be taken into account unless—
-- src:851 | (a) it is to be treated as income (see paragraphs (3) and (4)); or
-- src:852 | (b) it is to be disregarded (see regulation 48).
-- src:853 | (2) A person's personal possessions are not to be treated as capital.
-- src:854 | (3) Subject to paragraph (4), any sums that are paid regularly and by reference to a period, for example payments under an annuity, are to be treated as income even if they would, apart from this provision, be regarded as capital or as having a capital element.
-- src:855 | (4) Where capital is payable by instalments, each payment of an instalment is to be treated as income if the amount outstanding, combined with any other capital of the person (and, if the person is a member of a couple, the other member), exceeds £16,000, but otherwise such payments are to be treated as capital.
@ref reg 46 (src:850-855)
GIVEN sum IS A `Sum considered under regulation 46`
GIVETH A `Treatment of a sum under regulation 46`
`reg 46 — how a sum is treated` sum MEANS
    IF sum's `personal possession`
    THEN `not treated as capital because it is a personal possession`
    ELSE IF sum's `capital payable by instalments`
         THEN (IF `reg 46(4) — each instalment is treated as income` sum
               THEN `treated as income`
               ELSE `reg 46(1) — capital that is taken into account unless disregarded` sum)
         ELSE IF sum's `sum paid regularly and by reference to a period`
              THEN `treated as income`
              ELSE `reg 46(1) — capital that is taken into account unless disregarded` sum

-- src:850 | (1) The whole of a person's capital is to be taken into account unless—
-- src:851 | (a) it is to be treated as income (see paragraphs (3) and (4)); or
-- src:852 | (b) it is to be disregarded (see regulation 48).
@ref reg 46(1) (src:850-852)
GIVEN sum IS A `Sum considered under regulation 46`
GIVETH A `Treatment of a sum under regulation 46`
`reg 46(1) — capital that is taken into account unless disregarded` sum MEANS
    IF sum's `capital to be disregarded under regulation 48`
    THEN `capital that is disregarded`
    ELSE `capital that is taken into account`

§§ `Regulation 47 -- Jointly held capital`

-- Where a person and one or more others have a beneficial interest in a capital asset, they are treated, absent evidence to the
-- contrary, as each entitled to an equal share of the whole of that interest. The text does not say what share applies where there
-- is evidence to the contrary; the share that evidence shows is a plain input (fork F-CAP1).
-- src:856 | 47 Jointly held capital
-- src:857 | Where a person and one or more other persons have a beneficial interest in a capital asset, those persons are to be treated, in the absence of evidence to the contrary, as if they were each entitled to an equal share of the whole of that beneficial interest.
@ref reg 47 (src:856-857)
DECLARE `Jointly held capital asset` HAS
    `value of the whole of the beneficial interest`                       IS A NUMBER
    `number of persons with a beneficial interest, including the person`  IS A NUMBER
    `fraction of the whole that the evidence to the contrary shows is the person's share`  IS A MAYBE NUMBER

-- src:856 | 47 Jointly held capital
-- src:857 | Where a person and one or more other persons have a beneficial interest in a capital asset, those persons are to be treated, in the absence of evidence to the contrary, as if they were each entitled to an equal share of the whole of that beneficial interest.
@ref reg 47 (src:856-857)
GIVEN asset IS A `Jointly held capital asset`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`reg 47 — the value of the person's share of jointly held capital` asset MEANS
    CONSIDER asset's `fraction of the whole that the evidence to the contrary shows is the person's share`
    WHEN JUST share THEN JUST (`core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny` ((asset's `value of the whole of the beneficial interest`) TIMES share))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF asset's `number of persons with a beneficial interest, including the person` AT LEAST 1
        THEN JUST (`core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny` ((asset's `value of the whole of the beneficial interest`) DIVIDED BY (asset's `number of persons with a beneficial interest, including the person`)))
        ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Regulation 48 -- Capital disregarded`

-- Paragraph (1): capital specified in Schedule 10 is disregarded (see also regulations 75 to 77, which disregard other capital).
-- Schedule 10's paragraphs are in uc2-sch10-capital-disregards.l4, which decides whether the capital is "specified"; this rule
-- takes that result, and the results of regulations 75 to 77, as facts.
-- src:858 | 48 Capital disregarded
-- src:859 | (1) Any capital specified in Schedule 10 is to be disregarded from the calculation of a person's capital (see also regulations 75 to 77).
@ref reg 48(1) (src:858-859)
GIVEN `the capital is specified in a paragraph of Schedule 10` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reg 48(1) — the capital is disregarded` `the capital is specified in a paragraph of Schedule 10` MEANS
    `the capital is specified in a paragraph of Schedule 10`

-- Paragraph (2): where Schedule 10 specifies a period of 6 months, the Secretary of State may extend it where it is reasonable to do
-- so in the circumstances of the case. The extension is a judgment of the Secretary of State and the text names no limit, so the
-- length it is extended to is a conditional input; a figure under 6 is not an extension, so the period is never shorter than 6.
-- src:860 | (2) Where a period of 6 months is specified in that Schedule, that period may be extended by the Secretary of State where it is reasonable to do so in the circumstances of the case.
@ref reg 48(2) (src:860)
GIVEN `months to which the Secretary of State has extended the period of 6 months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A `Time limit`
`reg 48(2) — the period that Schedule 10 specifies as 6 months` `months to which the Secretary of State has extended the period of 6 months` MEANS
    `Time limit` WITH
        quantity IS (CONSIDER `months to which the Secretary of State has extended the period of 6 months`
                     WHEN NOTHING THEN 6
                     WHEN JUST extended THEN max 6 extended)
        unit     IS months

§§ `Regulation 49 -- Valuation of capital`

-- Paragraph (1): current market value or surrender value, less 10% where there would be expenses attributable to sale, and less
-- the amount of any encumbrances secured on it. Paragraph (2): an asset outside the United Kingdom is worth its market value in
-- that country if the transfer of an equal amount to the United Kingdom is not prohibited there, and otherwise what it would
-- raise if sold in the United Kingdom to a willing buyer. Paragraph (3): capital held in a currency other than sterling is
-- calculated after deducting any banking charge or commission payable in converting it. The order of (3) and (1) is fork F-CAP2.
-- src:862 | (1) Capital is to be calculated at its current market value or surrender value less—
-- src:863 | (a) where there would be expenses attributable to sale, 10%; and
-- src:864 | (b) the amount of any encumbrances secured on it.
-- src:865 | (2) The market value of a capital asset possessed by a person in a country outside the United Kingdom is—
-- src:866 | (a) if there is no prohibition in that country against the transfer of an amount equal to the value of that asset to the United Kingdom, the market value in that country; or
-- src:867 | (b) if there is such a prohibition, the amount it would raise if sold in the United Kingdom to a willing buyer.
-- src:868 | (3) Where capital is held in currency other than sterling, it is to be calculated after the deduction of any banking charge or commission payable in converting that capital into sterling.
@ref reg 49 (src:862-868)
DECLARE `Capital asset to be valued` HAS
    `current market value or surrender value of the asset (in the country where it is possessed, in sterling terms)`  IS A NUMBER
    `possessed in a country outside the United Kingdom`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `prohibition in that country against transferring an amount equal to the value to the United Kingdom`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `amount it would raise if sold in the United Kingdom to a willing buyer`  IS A NUMBER
    `there would be expenses attributable to sale`                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `amount of any encumbrances secured on it`                                IS A NUMBER
    `held in a currency other than sterling`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `banking charge or commission payable in converting it into sterling`     IS A NUMBER

-- src:865 | (2) The market value of a capital asset possessed by a person in a country outside the United Kingdom is—
-- src:866 | (a) if there is no prohibition in that country against the transfer of an amount equal to the value of that asset to the United Kingdom, the market value in that country; or
-- src:867 | (b) if there is such a prohibition, the amount it would raise if sold in the United Kingdom to a willing buyer.
@ref reg 49(2) (src:865-867)
GIVEN asset IS A `Capital asset to be valued`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 49(2) — the market value of the asset` asset MEANS
    IF asset's `possessed in a country outside the United Kingdom`
    THEN (IF asset's `prohibition in that country against transferring an amount equal to the value to the United Kingdom`
          THEN asset's `amount it would raise if sold in the United Kingdom to a willing buyer`
          ELSE asset's `current market value or surrender value of the asset (in the country where it is possessed, in sterling terms)`)
    ELSE asset's `current market value or surrender value of the asset (in the country where it is possessed, in sterling terms)`

-- src:862 | (1) Capital is to be calculated at its current market value or surrender value less—
-- src:863 | (a) where there would be expenses attributable to sale, 10%; and
-- src:864 | (b) the amount of any encumbrances secured on it.
-- src:865 | (2) The market value of a capital asset possessed by a person in a country outside the United Kingdom is—
-- src:866 | (a) if there is no prohibition in that country against the transfer of an amount equal to the value of that asset to the United Kingdom, the market value in that country; or
-- src:867 | (b) if there is such a prohibition, the amount it would raise if sold in the United Kingdom to a willing buyer.
-- src:868 | (3) Where capital is held in currency other than sterling, it is to be calculated after the deduction of any banking charge or commission payable in converting that capital into sterling.
@ref reg 49 (src:862-868)
GIVEN asset IS A `Capital asset to be valued`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 49 — the value of capital` asset MEANS
    `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny`
        (   (`reg 49(3) — the value after any banking charge on conversion` asset)
          MINUS (IF asset's `there would be expenses attributable to sale`
                 THEN 10% TIMES (`reg 49(3) — the value after any banking charge on conversion` asset)
                 ELSE 0)
          MINUS (asset's `amount of any encumbrances secured on it`))

-- src:868 | (3) Where capital is held in currency other than sterling, it is to be calculated after the deduction of any banking charge or commission payable in converting that capital into sterling.
@ref reg 49(3) (src:868)
GIVEN asset IS A `Capital asset to be valued`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 49(3) — the value after any banking charge on conversion` asset MEANS
    (`reg 49(2) — the market value of the asset` asset)
    MINUS (IF asset's `held in a currency other than sterling`
           THEN asset's `banking charge or commission payable in converting it into sterling`
           ELSE 0)

§§ `Regulation 50 -- Notional capital`

-- Paragraphs (1) and (2): a person is treated as possessing capital of which they have deprived themselves for the purpose of
-- securing entitlement to universal credit or to an increased amount of it; but not where they disposed of it to reduce or pay a
-- debt they owe, or to purchase goods or services if the expenditure was reasonable in their circumstances.
-- src:870 | (1) A person is to be treated as possessing capital of which the person has deprived themselves for the purpose of securing entitlement to universal credit or to an increased amount of universal credit.
-- src:871 | (2) A person is not to be treated as depriving themselves of capital if the person disposes of it for the purposes of—
-- src:872 | (a) reducing or paying a debt owed by the person; or
-- src:873 | (b) purchasing goods or services if the expenditure was reasonable in the circumstances of the person's case.
@ref reg 50(1) and (2) (src:870-873)
DECLARE `Disposal of capital considered under regulation 50` HAS
    `amount of the capital of which the person has deprived themselves`        IS A NUMBER
    `deprived themselves of the capital for the purpose of securing entitlement to universal credit or to an increased amount of universal credit`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `disposed of it for the purpose of reducing or paying a debt owed by the person`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `disposed of it for the purpose of purchasing goods or services`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the expenditure was reasonable in the circumstances of the person's case`  IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:871 | (2) A person is not to be treated as depriving themselves of capital if the person disposes of it for the purposes of—
-- src:872 | (a) reducing or paying a debt owed by the person; or
-- src:873 | (b) purchasing goods or services if the expenditure was reasonable in the circumstances of the person's case.
@ref reg 50(2) (src:871-873)
GIVEN disposal IS A `Disposal of capital considered under regulation 50`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reg 50(2) — the disposal is not treated as depriving the person of capital` disposal MEANS
        (disposal's `disposed of it for the purpose of reducing or paying a debt owed by the person`)
    OR  (    (disposal's `disposed of it for the purpose of purchasing goods or services`)
         AND (disposal's `the expenditure was reasonable in the circumstances of the person's case`))

-- src:870 | (1) A person is to be treated as possessing capital of which the person has deprived themselves for the purpose of securing entitlement to universal credit or to an increased amount of universal credit.
-- src:871 | (2) A person is not to be treated as depriving themselves of capital if the person disposes of it for the purposes of—
-- src:872 | (a) reducing or paying a debt owed by the person; or
-- src:873 | (b) purchasing goods or services if the expenditure was reasonable in the circumstances of the person's case.
@ref reg 50(1) and (2) (src:870-873)
GIVEN disposal IS A `Disposal of capital considered under regulation 50`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reg 50(1) — the person is treated as possessing the capital` disposal MEANS
        (disposal's `deprived themselves of the capital for the purpose of securing entitlement to universal credit or to an increased amount of universal credit`)
    AND (NOT (`reg 50(2) — the disposal is not treated as depriving the person of capital` disposal))

-- src:870 | (1) A person is to be treated as possessing capital of which the person has deprived themselves for the purpose of securing entitlement to universal credit or to an increased amount of universal credit.
-- src:871 | (2) A person is not to be treated as depriving themselves of capital if the person disposes of it for the purposes of—
-- src:872 | (a) reducing or paying a debt owed by the person; or
-- src:873 | (b) purchasing goods or services if the expenditure was reasonable in the circumstances of the person's case.
@ref reg 50(1) and (2) (src:870-873)
GIVEN disposal IS A `Disposal of capital considered under regulation 50`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 50(1) — the notional capital` disposal MEANS
    IF `reg 50(1) — the person is treated as possessing the capital` disposal
    THEN disposal's `amount of the capital of which the person has deprived themselves`
    ELSE 0

-- Paragraph (3): for each subsequent assessment period (or month, once the award has terminated) the notional capital reduces.
-- Over 16,000 pounds: by the amount of the award the Secretary of State considers would be made if it were not for the notional
-- capital (a judgment, so an input). Over 6,000 but not over 16,000 (including a sum that sub-paragraph (a) has brought down to 16,000
-- or less): by the unearned income it is treated as yielding under regulation 72. The text provides no reduction at 6,000 or less
-- (fork F-CAP3), so there the notional capital stays as it is.
-- src:874 | (3) Where a person is treated as possessing capital in accordance with this regulation, then for each subsequent assessment period (or, in a case where the award has terminated, each subsequent month) the amount of capital the person is treated as possessing (“the notional capital”) reduces—
-- src:875 | (a) in a case where the notional capital exceeds £16,000, by the amount which the Secretary of State considers would be the amount of an award of universal credit that would be made to the person (assuming they met the conditions in section 4 and 5 of the Act) if it were not for the notional capital; or
-- src:876 | (b) in a case where the notional capital exceeds £6,000 but not £16,000 (including where the notional capital has reduced to an amount equal to or less than £16,000 in accordance with sub-paragraph (a)) by the amount of unearned income that the notional capital is treated as yielding under regulation 72.
@ref reg 50(3) (src:874-876)
GIVEN `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month` IS A NUMBER
      `amount of the award of universal credit that the Secretary of State considers would be made if it were not for the notional capital` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 50(3) — the notional capital after an assessment period or month` `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month` `amount of the award of universal credit that the Secretary of State considers would be made if it were not for the notional capital` MEANS
    `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny`
        (IF `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month` GREATER THAN 16000
         THEN `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month` MINUS `amount of the award of universal credit that the Secretary of State considers would be made if it were not for the notional capital`
         ELSE (IF `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month` GREATER THAN 6000
               THEN `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month` MINUS (`reg 72(1) — the monthly income that capital is treated as yielding` `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month`)
               ELSE `notional capital at the start of the assessment period or month`))
